window.QBANK={"1":[{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-001","t":"Under IRC §7602(a)(1), the Secretary is authorized to examine books, papers, records, or other data that may be relevant or material to ascertaining the correctness of a return.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7602(a)(1)","rat":"§7602(a)(1) authorizes examining relevant records for tax audits."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-002","t":"Under IRC §7602(c)(1)(B), except as otherwise provided by the Secretary, notice that third parties may be contacted must be provided to the taxpayer not later than 90 days before the beginning of the specified period.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7602(c)(1)(B)","rat":"The statute requires 45 days' notice, not 90 days."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-003","t":"Under IRC §7602(d)(1), no summons may be issued, and the Secretary may not begin an action to enforce a summons, with respect to a person for whom a Justice Department referral is in effect.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7602(d)(1)","rat":"While a Justice Department referral is in effect, issuing or enforcing an administrative summons is prohibited."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-004","t":"Under IRC §7602(e), the Secretary may not use financial status or economic reality examination techniques to determine the existence of unreported income unless the Secretary has a reasonable indication of a likelihood of such unreported income.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7602(e)","rat":"Financial-status/economic-reality examination techniques may be used only if there is a reasonable indication of unreported income."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-005","t":"Under IRC §6212(d), the Secretary may rescind a notice of deficiency mailed to the taxpayer without needing the taxpayer's consent.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6212(d)","rat":"Withdrawing a notice of deficiency requires the taxpayer's consent."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-006","t":"Under IRC §6212(a), a notice of deficiency must be sent to the taxpayer by certified mail or registered mail.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6212(a)","rat":"A notice of deficiency must be sent by certified or registered mail."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-007","t":"Under IRC §6213(a), a taxpayer generally has 90 days after a notice of deficiency is mailed (150 days if the notice is addressed to a person outside the United States) to file a petition with the Tax Court for redetermination of the deficiency.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6213(a)","rat":"The Tax Court petition deadline is 90 days (150 days for a taxpayer outside the U.S.)."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-008","t":"Under IRC §6213(b)(1), a notice regarding a mathematical or clerical error is treated as a notice of deficiency, entitling the taxpayer to file a petition with the Tax Court based on that notice.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6213(b)(1)","rat":"A math or clerical error notice is not a notice of deficiency, so it gives no right to petition the Tax Court."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-009","t":"Under IRC §6213(a), once a taxpayer files a timely petition with the Tax Court, no assessment of the deficiency may be made until the decision of the Tax Court becomes final, subject to certain statutory exceptions.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6213(a)","rat":"Once a timely petition is filed, assessment is generally barred until the Tax Court decision becomes final (with exceptions)."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-010","t":"Under IRC §6212(c)(1), even absent fraud, after mailing a notice of deficiency and the taxpayer's timely petition to the Tax Court, the Secretary retains unrestricted authority to determine additional deficiencies for the same taxable year.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6212(c)(1)","rat":"Absent fraud, a second notice of deficiency for the same tax year is generally barred."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-011","t":"Under IRC §7442, the Tax Court and its divisions have such jurisdiction as is conferred by Title 26 and certain other specified statutes.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7442","rat":"The Tax Court's jurisdiction is granted by Title 26 and related statutes."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-012","t":"Under IRC §7482(a)(1), the United States Courts of Appeals (other than the Federal Circuit) have exclusive jurisdiction to review decisions of the Tax Court, in the same manner as decisions of district courts in civil actions tried without a jury.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7482(a)(1)","rat":"The U.S. Courts of Appeals (other than the Federal Circuit) have exclusive review jurisdiction over Tax Court decisions."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-013","t":"Under IRC §7482(a)(1), decisions of the United States Courts of Appeals reviewing Tax Court decisions are final and are not subject to review by the Supreme Court under any circumstances.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7482(a)(1)","rat":"A court of appeals decision may be reviewed by the Supreme Court on certiorari."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-014","t":"Under IRC §7483, review of a Tax Court decision is generally obtained by filing a notice of appeal with the clerk of the Tax Court within 90 days after the decision is entered.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7483","rat":"An appeal from a Tax Court decision requires filing a notice of appeal with the Tax Court clerk within 90 days of the decision."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-015","t":"Under IRC §7483, if one party files a timely notice of appeal, any other party may take an appeal by filing a notice of appeal within 120 days after the Tax Court's decision is entered.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7483","rat":"If the other party appeals first, the remaining party may file a notice of appeal within 120 days."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-016","t":"Under IRC §7482(b)(2), the venue for review of a Tax Court decision can never be changed by agreement between the Secretary and the taxpayer and is fixed solely by the general venue rules.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7482(b)(2)","rat":"The Secretary and the taxpayer may designate a different court of appeals by written stipulation."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-017","t":"Under IRC §7482(c)(1), upon review, a Court of Appeals has the power to affirm, or, if the decision of the Tax Court is not in accordance with law, to modify or reverse the decision, with or without remanding the case for a rehearing.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7482(c)(1)","rat":"A court of appeals may affirm, modify, or reverse a Tax Court decision and remand for further proceedings."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-018","t":"Under IRC §7482(c)(4), a Court of Appeals has no power to impose a penalty on a taxpayer even where the Tax Court's decision is affirmed and the appeal was instituted primarily for delay.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7482(c)(4)","rat":"If an appeal is frivolous or brought merely to delay, both the court of appeals and the Supreme Court may impose a penalty."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-019","t":"Under IRC §7482(b)(1)(A), for an individual (non-corporate) petitioner seeking redetermination of tax liability, venue for review generally lies in the circuit of the petitioner's legal residence.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §7482(b)(1)(A)","rat":"For an individual petitioner, review is generally in the court of appeals for the circuit of the taxpayer's legal residence."},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-001","t":"Which of the following statements about the IRS summons authority under IRC §7602 is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements about the IRS summons authority under IRC §7602 is correct?","d":"","o":["The Secretary may use financial status or economic reality examination techniques to search for unreported income regardless of any prior indication that such income exists.","A summons issued under §7602 need not be relevant or material to determining the taxpayer's liability.","No summons may be issued under Title 26, and the Secretary may not begin an action to enforce a summons, with respect to a person for whom a Justice Department referral is in effect.","The Secretary may contact any third party regarding a taxpayer's liability at any time without any prior notice to the taxpayer."],"a":3,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §7602(d)(1)","rat":"While a Justice Department referral is in effect, issuing or enforcing a summons is prohibited."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-002","t":"Which of the following statements regarding the notice of deficiency process is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements regarding the notice of deficiency process is correct?","d":"","o":["Once a notice of deficiency is mailed, the Secretary may immediately levy to collect the deficiency regardless of whether the taxpayer files a Tax Court petition.","A notice of deficiency may only be sent to the taxpayer by first-class mail.","A taxpayer generally has 90 days after a notice of deficiency is mailed to file a petition with the Tax Court, or 150 days if the notice is addressed to a person outside the United States.","The Secretary may rescind a notice of deficiency without the consent of the taxpayer."],"a":3,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6213(a)","rat":"The Tax Court petition deadline is 90 days (150 days for a taxpayer outside the U.S.)."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-003","t":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct regarding restrictions on additional deficiency notices?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct regarding restrictions on additional deficiency notices?","d":"","o":["Under §6212(c)(1), after mailing a notice of deficiency and the taxpayer's timely petition to the Tax Court, the Secretary generally may not determine an additional deficiency of income tax for the same taxable year.","A notice regarding a mathematical or clerical error under §6213(b)(1) is treated as a notice of deficiency for purposes of the restriction on further deficiency letters under §6212(c)(1).","An exception to the restriction on additional deficiency notices applies in the case of fraud.","Under §6213(d), a taxpayer may waive the restrictions on assessment and collection of a deficiency at any time by a signed notice in writing filed with the Secretary."],"a":2,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6213(b)(1)","rat":"A math or clerical error notice is not treated as a notice of deficiency."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-004","t":"Which of the following statements about appellate review of Tax Court decisions is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements about appellate review of Tax Court decisions is correct?","d":"","o":["Venue for review of a Tax Court decision can never be changed regardless of any agreement between the Secretary and the taxpayer.","The United States Courts of Appeals, other than the Federal Circuit, have exclusive jurisdiction to review decisions of the Tax Court, subject to review by the Supreme Court upon certiorari.","Decisions of the Courts of Appeals reviewing Tax Court decisions can never be reviewed by the Supreme Court under any circumstances.","Tax Court decisions may only be reviewed by the Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit."],"a":2,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §7482(a)(1)","rat":"A court of appeals decision may be reviewed by the Supreme Court on certiorari."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-005","t":"Which of the following statements about filing a notice of appeal from a Tax Court decision under IRC §7483 is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements about filing a notice of appeal from a Tax Court decision under IRC §7483 is correct?","d":"","o":["A notice of appeal must be filed with the clerk of the Tax Court within 30 days after the decision is entered.","There is no time limit on filing a notice of appeal from a Tax Court decision.","A notice of appeal from a Tax Court decision must be filed directly with the Supreme Court.","If one party files a timely notice of appeal, any other party has 120 days after the Tax Court's decision is entered to file its own notice of appeal."],"a":4,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §7483","rat":"If one party appeals first, the other party may file a notice of appeal within 120 days."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-006","t":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct regarding review of Tax Court decisions?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct regarding review of Tax Court decisions?","d":"","o":["Venue for review may be changed by written stipulation between the Secretary and the taxpayer designating a different Court of Appeals.","The Tax Court's jurisdiction to hear a case derives solely from the U.S. Constitution and not from any grant of jurisdiction under Title 26.","A Court of Appeals may require a taxpayer to pay a penalty to the United States if the Tax Court's decision is affirmed and the appeal was instituted primarily for delay.","Upon review, a Court of Appeals may affirm, modify, or reverse a Tax Court decision that is not in accordance with law."],"a":2,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §7442","rat":"The Tax Court's jurisdiction is granted by Title 26 and related statutes."}}],"2":[{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-020","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(i), the amount of an understatement is reduced for the tax treatment of an item if there is or was substantial authority for such treatment, regardless of whether it was disclosed on the return.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(i)","rat":"If substantial authority exists, the understatement is reduced regardless of disclosure."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-021","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii), the amount of an understatement is reduced for an item if the relevant facts affecting its tax treatment are adequately disclosed in the return (or in a statement attached to the return) and there is a reasonable basis for the taxpayer's treatment of the item.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)","rat":"The understatement reduction requires both adequate disclosure and a reasonable basis, together."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-022","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(C)(i), the disclosure-based reduction of an understatement applies even to items attributable to a tax shelter.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(C)(i)","rat":"Items attributable to a tax shelter are not eligible for the disclosure-based reduction."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-023","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(II), disclosure of the relevant facts alone, without any reasonable basis for the tax treatment of the item, is sufficient for the understatement reduction to apply.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(II)","rat":"Disclosure alone is not enough; a reasonable basis for the position is also required."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-024","t":"Under IRC §6662(i), disclosing the relevant facts of a transaction described in §6662(b)(6) in the return has no effect on the penalty rate applicable to an underpayment attributable to that transaction.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(i)","rat":"An undisclosed transaction lacking economic substance is subject to a heightened 40% penalty rate."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-025","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(3), the Secretary may prescribe and publish, in the Federal Register or the Internal Revenue Bulletin, a list of positions believed not to meet the substantial authority or disclosure standards.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(3)","rat":"The Secretary may publish, e.g., in the Federal Register, a list of positions found not to meet the required standard."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-027","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(II), a corporation may be treated as having a reasonable basis for its tax treatment of an item attributable to a multiple-party financing transaction even if such treatment does not clearly reflect the corporation's income.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(II)","rat":"A multiple-party financing arrangement that fails to clearly reflect the entity's income does not qualify as a reasonable basis."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-028","t":"A taxpayer who has substantial authority for the tax treatment of an item does not need to disclose that item on the return in order to obtain a reduction in the understatement under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(i).","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(i)","rat":"With substantial authority, the understatement reduction applies even without disclosure."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-029","t":"A taxpayer who lacks substantial authority for an item's tax treatment can still reduce the understatement attributable to that item by adequately disclosing the relevant facts on the return (or an attached statement) if there is a reasonable basis for the treatment.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)","rat":"Without substantial authority, the reduction is still available with adequate disclosure and a reasonable basis."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-030","t":"A taxpayer engaged in a tax shelter arrangement can avoid contributing to a substantial understatement for an item attributable to that tax shelter merely by disclosing the item on the return.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(C)(i)","rat":"Items attributable to a tax shelter cannot get the reduction through disclosure alone."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-031","t":"For a transaction described in IRC §6662(b)(6) (lacking economic substance), the relevant facts must be adequately disclosed in the return or an attached statement to avoid the increased 40 percent penalty rate under §6662(i).","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(i)(1)-(2)","rat":"A transaction lacking economic substance is subject to the 40% penalty rate if not disclosed."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-032","t":"Disclosure of an item's relevant facts on the return (or an attached statement) is required for every item, regardless of whether substantial authority exists, in order to avoid the substantial understatement penalty under IRC §6662.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(i)","rat":"Items with substantial authority can get the reduction even without disclosure."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-033","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(I), disclosure for purposes of the understatement reduction may be made in a statement attached to the return, not necessarily on the face of the return itself.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(I)","rat":"Disclosure can be made either on the return itself or in an attached statement."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-034","t":"A corporation may rely on disclosure and a claimed reasonable basis for an item involving a multiple-party financing transaction even where the tax treatment does not clearly reflect the corporation's income.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(II)","rat":"A multiple-party financing arrangement that fails to clearly reflect corporate income is not a reasonable basis."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-035","t":"Positions on the Secretary's published list under IRC §6662(d)(3) are ones the Secretary believes fail to meet the substantial authority or adequate-disclosure-with-reasonable-basis standards.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(3)","rat":"The Secretary's published list covers positions that fail the substantial-authority or disclosure-plus-reasonable-basis standards."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-036","t":"Under IRC §6001, every person liable for any tax must keep such records, render such statements, make such returns, and comply with such rules and regulations as the Secretary may prescribe.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6001","rat":"§6001 sets the taxpayer's general recordkeeping and filing requirements."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-037","t":"Under IRC §274(d), no deduction is allowed for a traveling expense (including meals and lodging while away from home) unless the taxpayer substantiates the amount, the time and place of the travel, the business purpose, and the business relationship of the person receiving the benefit, by adequate records or sufficient corroborating evidence.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §274(d)","rat":"A travel expense deduction requires substantiating amount, time and place, business purpose, and business relationship."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-038","t":"Under IRC §274(d), the heightened substantiation requirement (amount, time/place, business purpose, and business relationship) applies not only to travel expenses but also to gifts and to listed property.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §274(d)","rat":"The strict substantiation rules of §274(d) apply not only to travel but also to gifts and listed property."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-039","t":"Under IRC §274(d), a taxpayer's own uncorroborated oral statement, without adequate records or other corroborating evidence, is sufficient to substantiate a travel expense deduction.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §274(d)","rat":"The taxpayer's own statement alone is not enough; adequate records or corroborating evidence are required."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-040","t":"Under IRC §274(d), the Secretary may by regulation provide that some or all of the substantiation requirements do not apply to an expense that does not exceed an amount prescribed in those regulations.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §274(d)","rat":"The Secretary may, by regulation, waive part of the substantiation requirement for expenditures below a set amount."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-041","t":"Under IRC §274(d), the heightened substantiation requirements apply to a qualified nonpersonal use vehicle in the same manner as to other listed property.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §274(d)","rat":"§274(d) expressly does not apply to a qualified nonpersonal use vehicle."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-042","t":"Under IRC §274(a)(1), no deduction is generally allowed for an activity of a type generally considered entertainment, amusement, or recreation, or for a facility used in connection with such an activity, subject to specific exceptions listed elsewhere in the section.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §274(a)(1)","rat":"Entertainment, amusement, and recreation expenses are generally nondeductible, subject to specifically listed exceptions."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-043","t":"Under IRC §6001, the Secretary may require additional records, statements, or returns only through formal regulations; a notice served directly on a specific person cannot impose such a requirement.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6001","rat":"§6001 lets the Secretary require additional records or returns by regulation or by notice served on a specific person."},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-007","t":"Which of the following is correct regarding the disclosure-based reduction of a substantial understatement under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is correct regarding the disclosure-based reduction of a substantial understatement under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)?","d":"","o":["An item's understatement amount is reduced if the relevant facts are adequately disclosed on the return (or an attached statement) and there is a reasonable basis for the taxpayer's tax treatment of the item.","Disclosure must be made to the Secretary within 30 days of filing the return in order to qualify for the reduction.","The disclosure-based reduction applies even to items attributable to a tax shelter.","Disclosure alone, without a reasonable basis for the position, is sufficient to reduce the understatement."],"a":1,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)","rat":"Both adequate disclosure and a reasonable basis are required together."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-008","t":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct under IRC §6662?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct under IRC §6662?","d":"","o":["A corporation can be treated as having a reasonable basis for its tax treatment of an item involving a multiple-party financing transaction even if the treatment does not clearly reflect the corporation's income.","Substantial authority for an item's tax treatment can reduce the understatement even without disclosure.","The Secretary may publish a list of positions believed not to meet the substantial authority or disclosure standards.","Items attributable to a tax shelter are excluded from the disclosure-based reduction of an understatement."],"a":1,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(II)","rat":"A multiple-party financing arrangement that fails to clearly reflect corporate income does not qualify as a reasonable basis."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-009","t":"Which of the following statements about the increased penalty for nondisclosed noneconomic substance transactions under IRC §6662(i) is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements about the increased penalty for nondisclosed noneconomic substance transactions under IRC §6662(i) is correct?","d":"","o":["The increased rate applies regardless of whether the transaction lacks economic substance under §7701(o).","The rate is increased from 20 percent to 40 percent for the portion of an underpayment attributable to a nondisclosed noneconomic substance transaction.","Disclosure of the relevant facts on the return has no effect on the applicable penalty rate for such a transaction.","The rate is increased from 20 percent to 75 percent for such a transaction."],"a":2,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(i)(1)","rat":"An undisclosed transaction lacking economic substance is subject to a heightened 40% penalty rate."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-010","t":"A taxpayer takes a tax position for which there is no substantial authority. Which of the following, if true, would allow the taxpayer to reduce the understatement attributable to that item under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"A taxpayer takes a tax position for which there is no substantial authority. Which of the following, if true, would allow the taxpayer to reduce the understatement attributable to that item under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)?","d":"","o":["The taxpayer merely believed the position was correct at the time of filing.","The item is attributable to a tax shelter and the taxpayer disclosed it on the return.","The taxpayer's return preparer, but not the taxpayer, was aware that the position was weak.","The taxpayer disclosed the relevant facts affecting the item's tax treatment on the return and had a reasonable basis for the treatment."],"a":4,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)","rat":"The reduction is available when adequate disclosure and a reasonable basis exist together."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-011","t":"Which of the following statements about disclosure requirements under IRC §6662 is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements about disclosure requirements under IRC §6662 is correct?","d":"","o":["An item with substantial authority for its tax treatment must still be disclosed to avoid contributing to a substantial understatement.","Disclosure of an item in a statement attached to the return never satisfies the disclosure requirement; only disclosure on the face of the return form qualifies.","An item without substantial authority may still avoid contributing to a substantial understatement if it is adequately disclosed and has a reasonable basis.","An item attributable to a tax shelter can achieve the same disclosure-based reduction as a non-tax-shelter item."],"a":3,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(d)(2)(B)","rat":"Even without substantial authority, the reduction is available with adequate disclosure and a reasonable basis."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-012","t":"Which of the following statements about substantiation of travel expenses under IRC §274(d) is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements about substantiation of travel expenses under IRC §274(d) is correct?","d":"","o":["Substantiation must address the amount of the expense, the time and place of the travel (or date and description of a gift), the business purpose, and the business relationship of the person receiving the benefit.","The Secretary has no authority to waive any of the §274(d) substantiation requirements by regulation.","The taxpayer's own statement alone, without adequate records or other corroborating evidence, is sufficient substantiation.","Substantiation requirements under §274(d) never apply to gifts, only to travel expenses."],"a":1,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §274(d)","rat":"§274(d) requires substantiating amount, time and place, business purpose, and business relationship."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-013","t":"Which of the following is NOT correct regarding recordkeeping under IRC §6001?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is NOT correct regarding recordkeeping under IRC §6001?","d":"","o":["The Secretary may require additional returns, statements, or records by regulation.","The Secretary may also require additional returns, statements, or records by a notice served on a specific person.","Every person liable for a tax imposed by Title 26 must keep such records as the Secretary may prescribe.","A taxpayer's recordkeeping obligation under §6001 exists only if the taxpayer has been individually notified by the Secretary; general regulations cannot impose such an obligation."],"a":4,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6001","rat":"§6001 allows the recordkeeping requirement to be imposed either by regulation or by an individual notice."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-014","t":"Which of the following expenditures is subject to the heightened substantiation rule of IRC §274(d)?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following expenditures is subject to the heightened substantiation rule of IRC §274(d)?","d":"","o":["A deduction for a business gift.","A deduction for mortgage interest expense.","A deduction for state income taxes paid.","A deduction for a casualty loss."],"a":1,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §274(d)(2)","rat":"A deduction for a gift is one of the items subject to the strict substantiation rules of §274(d)."}}],"3":[{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-044","t":"Under IRC §6651(a)(1), a failure to file a return described in that paragraph by its due date (including extensions) generally results in an addition to tax of 5 percent of the tax required to be shown for each month or partial month the failure continues, up to a maximum of 25 percent, unless the failure is due to reasonable cause and not willful neglect.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(a)(1)","rat":"The failure-to-file penalty is 5% per month, up to 25%, waived for reasonable cause."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-045","t":"Under IRC §6651(a)(2), a failure to pay the amount shown as tax on a return by its payment due date generally results in an addition to tax of 0.5 percent of that amount per month (or fraction thereof), up to a maximum of 25 percent, absent reasonable cause.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(a)(2)","rat":"The failure-to-pay penalty is 0.5% per month, up to 25%."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-046","t":"Under IRC §6651(f), if a failure to file a return is fraudulent, the percentages under §6651(a)(1) remain unchanged at 5 percent per month and 25 percent maximum.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(f)","rat":"For fraudulent failure to file, the rate rises to 15% per month, up to 75%."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-047","t":"Under IRC §6663(a), if any part of an underpayment of tax is due to fraud, there is added to the tax an amount equal to 75 percent of the portion of the underpayment which is attributable to fraud.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6663(a)","rat":"The fraud penalty is 75% of the underpayment attributable to fraud."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-048","t":"Under IRC §6663(b), if the Secretary establishes that any portion of an underpayment is attributable to fraud, the entire underpayment is treated as attributable to fraud, except for any portion the taxpayer establishes, by a preponderance of the evidence, is not attributable to fraud.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6663(b)","rat":"If any part is shown to be fraud, the entire underpayment is presumed fraudulent, but the taxpayer may rebut this."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-049","t":"Under IRC §6663(c), on a joint return, the fraud penalty applies to both spouses even if only one spouse committed the fraud and no part of the underpayment is due to the other spouse's fraud.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6663(c)","rat":"On a joint return, a spouse with no fraud of their own is not subject to the fraud penalty for that portion."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-050","t":"Under IRC §6662(b)(1), the 20 percent accuracy-related penalty applies to the portion of an underpayment attributable to negligence or disregard of rules or regulations.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(a), (b)(1)","rat":"An underpayment due to negligence or disregard of rules is subject to a 20% accuracy-related penalty."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-051","t":"Under IRC §6662(a), the accuracy-related penalty under §6662 may be imposed on the same portion of an underpayment on which a fraud penalty is imposed under §6663.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(b) (last sentence)","rat":"The §6662 accuracy-related penalty does not apply to a portion already subject to the §6663 fraud penalty."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-052","t":"Under IRC §6651(a)(3), a failure to pay an amount stated in a notice and demand within 21 calendar days of the notice and demand (10 business days if the amount equals or exceeds $100,000) can also give rise to an addition to tax, absent reasonable cause.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(a)(3)","rat":"A payment delay after notice and demand is also a separate ground for penalty."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-053","t":"Under IRC §6651(g), a return prepared by the Secretary under §6020(b) is disregarded for purposes of determining the failure-to-file addition under §6651(a)(1), but is treated as the taxpayer's filed return for purposes of the failure-to-pay additions under §6651(a)(2) and (3).","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(g)","rat":"A return the Secretary prepares for the taxpayer (SFR) is disregarded for the failure-to-file penalty but is treated as a return for the failure-to-pay penalty."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-054","t":"A taxpayer who fails to pay the amount shown as tax on a timely filed return, without reasonable cause, is subject to an addition to tax under IRC §6651(a)(2) of 0.5 percent per month of the unpaid amount, up to 25 percent.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(a)(2)","rat":"Even with a timely filed return, a delayed payment draws a 0.5%-per-month failure-to-pay penalty."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-055","t":"A taxpayer who fails to both file a return and pay the tax shown, without reasonable cause, is subject to the full, unreduced amount of both the failure-to-file addition and the failure-to-pay addition for the same month, with no offset between them.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(c)(1)","rat":"When both penalties apply for the same month, the failure-to-file penalty is reduced by the failure-to-pay penalty amount."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-056","t":"A taxpayer whose underpayment is due to a substantial understatement of income tax may be subject to a 20 percent accuracy-related penalty under IRC §6662, unless an exception (such as adequate disclosure with a reasonable basis, or substantial authority) applies.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(a), (b)(2), (d)","rat":"A substantial understatement draws a 20% penalty, subject to exceptions such as disclosure and reasonable basis."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-057","t":"A taxpayer whose underpayment is due to fraud is subject to a 75 percent penalty under IRC §6663 on the portion of the underpayment attributable to the fraud, rather than the 20 percent penalty under §6662.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6663(a); §6662(b) (last sentence)","rat":"The portion attributable to fraud draws the 75% fraud penalty, not the accuracy-related penalty."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-058","t":"A taxpayer's failure to pay tax is never excused under IRC §6651; the addition to tax under §6651(a) applies regardless of the taxpayer's reason for the failure.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(a)","rat":"No penalty applies if the taxpayer shows reasonable cause and no willful neglect."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-059","t":"A married couple filing a joint return will not be subject to the fraud penalty under IRC §6663 with respect to a spouse unless some part of the underpayment is due to that spouse's own fraud.","ox":"O","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6663(c)","rat":"On a joint return, each spouse is liable for the fraud penalty only for their own fraudulent portion."},{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-060","t":"An individual taxpayer who files a return on time and enters into an installment agreement under IRC §6159 for payment of the tax owed cannot receive any reduction to the failure-to-pay penalty rate during the period the installment agreement is in effect.","ox":"X","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG","qn":null,"c":"A","cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(h)","rat":"During an installment agreement, the failure-to-pay rate drops from 0.5% to 0.25% per month."},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-015","t":"Which of the following statements about the fraud penalty under IRC §6663 is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following statements about the fraud penalty under IRC §6663 is correct?","d":"","o":["Once the Secretary establishes fraud as to any portion of the underpayment, the taxpayer has no opportunity to show that any portion is not attributable to fraud.","On a joint return, the fraud penalty applies to a spouse even absent any fraud by that spouse.","The fraud penalty equals 75 percent of the portion of the underpayment attributable to fraud.","The §6663 fraud penalty and the §6662 accuracy-related penalty may both be imposed on the same portion of an underpayment."],"a":3,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6663(a)","rat":"The fraud penalty is 75% of the underpayment attributable to fraud."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-016","t":"Which of the following is NOT correct regarding the failure-to-file addition to tax under IRC §6651(a)(1)?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is NOT correct regarding the failure-to-file addition to tax under IRC §6651(a)(1)?","d":"","o":["The addition generally equals 5 percent of the tax required to be shown per month or fraction thereof, up to 25 percent.","The addition does not apply if the failure is due to reasonable cause and not willful neglect.","If the failure to file is fraudulent, the percentages are increased to 15 percent per month and 75 percent maximum.","The failure-to-file addition under §6651(a)(1) applies to every type of return required under Title 26, with no limitation to the returns specified in that paragraph."],"a":4,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(a)(1)","rat":"§6651(a)(1) applies to failure to file the specific returns listed in that subsection (e.g., Subtitle A returns)."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-017","t":"A taxpayer underpays tax due to negligence, and no fraud is established. Which of the following penalties could apply to the underpayment?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"A taxpayer underpays tax due to negligence, and no fraud is established. Which of the following penalties could apply to the underpayment?","d":"","o":["No penalty at all can be imposed under §6651, §6662, or §6663 for mere negligence.","Both the full §6662 and the full §6663 penalties simultaneously on the same underpayment.","Only the §6663 fraud penalty.","The §6662 accuracy-related penalty for negligence or disregard of rules or regulations."],"a":4,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6662(a), (b)(1)","rat":"An underpayment due to negligence or disregard of rules draws a 20% accuracy-related penalty."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-018","t":"A taxpayer timely files a return but fails to pay the tax shown on the return, and there is no reasonable cause for the failure to pay. Which of the following is correct?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"A taxpayer timely files a return but fails to pay the tax shown on the return, and there is no reasonable cause for the failure to pay. Which of the following is correct?","d":"","o":["An addition to tax under §6651(a)(2) generally applies at 0.5 percent of the unpaid tax per month or fraction thereof, up to a maximum of 25 percent.","No addition to tax can apply because the return itself was timely filed.","The addition to tax is automatically 75 percent because fraud is presumed whenever tax is unpaid.","The addition to tax under §6651(a)(2) is entirely eliminated once the taxpayer enters into any agreement with the IRS regarding payment."],"a":1,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6651(a)(2)","rat":"The failure-to-pay penalty is 0.5% per month, up to 25%."}},{"i":"REGMC-reg_tax_procedures-019","t":"A married couple files a joint return. The IRS establishes that a portion of the underpayment is due to the fraud of the husband only. Which of the following is correct regarding the wife's liability for the §6663 fraud penalty?","c":"A","mc":{"s":"A married couple files a joint return. The IRS establishes that a portion of the underpayment is due to the fraud of the husband only. Which of the following is correct regarding the wife's liability for the §6663 fraud penalty?","d":"","o":["The fraud penalty automatically converts to the §6662 negligence penalty as applied to the wife.","The wife is automatically liable for the fraud penalty on the entire underpayment because the return was filed jointly.","The wife can never be held liable for any addition to tax on a jointly filed return.","The fraud penalty under §6663 does not apply to the wife unless some part of the underpayment is due to her own fraud."],"a":4,"cite":"26 U.S.C. §6663(c)","rat":"On a joint return, each spouse is liable for the fraud penalty only for their own fraudulent portion."}}]};
window.PAIRS=[{"n":1,"s":0.326,"o":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-006","t":"Under IRC §6212(a), a notice of deficiency must be sent to the taxpayer by certified mail or registered mail.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"},"x":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-005","t":"Under IRC §6212(d), the Secretary may rescind a notice of deficiency mailed to the taxpayer without needing the taxpayer's consent.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"}},{"n":2,"s":0.372,"o":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-021","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii), the amount of an understatement is reduced for an item if the relevant facts affecting its tax treatment are adequately disclosed in the return (or in a statement attached to the return) and there is a reasonable basis for the taxpayer's treatment of the item.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"},"x":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-023","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(ii)(II), disclosure of the relevant facts alone, without any reasonable basis for the tax treatment of the item, is sufficient for the understatement reduction to apply.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"}},{"n":2,"s":0.329,"o":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-029","t":"A taxpayer who lacks substantial authority for an item's tax treatment can still reduce the understatement attributable to that item by adequately disclosing the relevant facts on the return (or an attached statement) if there is a reasonable basis for the treatment.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"},"x":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-030","t":"A taxpayer engaged in a tax shelter arrangement can avoid contributing to a substantial understatement for an item attributable to that tax shelter merely by disclosing the item on the return.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"}},{"n":2,"s":0.322,"o":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-020","t":"Under IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)(i), the amount of an understatement is reduced for the tax treatment of an item if there is or was substantial authority for such treatment, regardless of whether it was disclosed on the return.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"},"x":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-032","t":"Disclosure of an item's relevant facts on the return (or an attached statement) is required for every item, regardless of whether substantial authority exists, in order to avoid the substantial understatement penalty under IRC §6662.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"}},{"n":2,"s":0.321,"o":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-031","t":"For a transaction described in IRC §6662(b)(6) (lacking economic substance), the relevant facts must be adequately disclosed in the return or an attached statement to avoid the increased 40 percent penalty rate under §6662(i).","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"},"x":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-024","t":"Under IRC §6662(i), disclosing the relevant facts of a transaction described in §6662(b)(6) in the return has no effect on the penalty rate applicable to an underpayment attributable to that transaction.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"}},{"n":3,"s":0.324,"o":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-054","t":"A taxpayer who fails to pay the amount shown as tax on a timely filed return, without reasonable cause, is subject to an addition to tax under IRC §6651(a)(2) of 0.5 percent per month of the unpaid amount, up to 25 percent.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"},"x":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-055","t":"A taxpayer who fails to both file a return and pay the tax shown, without reasonable cause, is subject to the full, unreduced amount of both the failure-to-file addition and the failure-to-pay addition for the same month, with no offset between them.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"}},{"n":3,"s":0.324,"o":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-059","t":"A married couple filing a joint return will not be subject to the fraud penalty under IRC §6663 with respect to a spouse unless some part of the underpayment is due to that spouse's own fraud.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"},"x":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-049","t":"Under IRC §6663(c), on a joint return, the fraud penalty applies to both spouses even if only one spouse committed the fraud and no part of the underpayment is due to the other spouse's fraud.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"}},{"n":3,"s":0.306,"o":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-050","t":"Under IRC §6662(b)(1), the 20 percent accuracy-related penalty applies to the portion of an underpayment attributable to negligence or disregard of rules or regulations.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"},"x":{"i":"REGOX-reg_tax_procedures-051","t":"Under IRC §6662(a), the accuracy-related penalty under §6662 may be imposed on the same portion of an underpayment on which a fraud penalty is imposed under §6663.","y":null,"e":"US CPA REG"}}];
